PART A — Maestro's beans suppress Spring Boot's, so spring.kafka.* is inert.
Boot's conditions, read out of spring-boot-kafka-4.0.5 bytecode:

$ javap -v KafkaAutoConfiguration.class   # kafkaTemplate(...)
        RuntimeVisibleAnnotations:
          0: #434()
            org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean
          1: #435(#436=[c#447])
            org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnMissingBean(
    --

$ javap -v KafkaAutoConfiguration.class   # kafkaProducerFactory(...)
        RuntimeVisibleAnnotations:
          0: #434()
            org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean
          1: #435(#436=[c#441])
            org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnMissingBean(
              value=[class Lorg/springframework/kafka/core/ProducerFactory;]
            )

Both are @ConditionalOnMissingBean on the TYPE, and Maestro contributes a
bean of each type unconditionally, so Boot's never exist in a Maestro+Kafka
app and the properties they read are never applied.

Maestro's replacements (no observation, no spring.kafka.* binding):
$ sed -n '93,110p' maestro-messaging-kafka/.../config/KafkaMessagingAutoConfiguration.java
        @Bean
        @ConditionalOnMissingBean(name = "maestroKafkaProducerFactory")
        public ProducerFactory<String, byte[]> maestroKafkaProducerFactory(Environment env) {
            var props = new HashMap<String, Object>();
            props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, resolveBootstrapServers(env));
            props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class);
            props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, ByteArraySerializer.class);
            props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
            return new DefaultKafkaProducerFactory<>(props);
        }
    
        @Bean
        @ConditionalOnMissingBean(name = "maestroKafkaTemplate")
        public KafkaTemplate<String, byte[]> maestroKafkaTemplate(
                ProducerFactory<String, byte[]> maestroKafkaProducerFactory
        ) {
            return new KafkaTemplate<>(maestroKafkaProducerFactory);
        }

PART B — the @MaestroSignalListener container is hand-built, so
spring.kafka.listener.* cannot reach it and no trace context is extracted.
$ sed -n '213,220p' maestro-messaging-kafka/.../listener/MaestroSignalListenerBeanPostProcessor.java
            var containerProps = new ContainerProperties(reg.topic());
            containerProps.setGroupId(groupId);
            containerProps.setAckMode(ContainerProperties.AckMode.RECORD);
            containerProps.setMessageListener(
                    (MessageListener<String, byte[]>) record -> handleMessage(record.value(), reg, executor, objectMapper));
    
            var container = new ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer<>(consumerFactory, containerProps);
            // Without this the default error handler logs and skips once its

The listener lambda calls handleMessage(...) directly. KafkaTracePropagation
exposes runWithExtractedContext(Headers, Runnable) for exactly this hop and
is never called here; setObservationEnabled is never set on containerProps.
$ grep -c runWithExtractedContext <the BPP>
0

PART C — observable consequence, measured on the running demo.
The producer side IS injecting traceparent here (the sample override is in
place; see task-2-jaeger-cross-service-trace.log for the header on the wire),
so any NULL below is the CONSUMER side dropping it.

$ psql loan_application -c 'SELECT signal_name, count(*), count(trace_context) ...'
          signal_name      | rows | with_trace | null_trace 
    -----------------------+------+------------+------------
     document.uploaded     |    1 |          0 |          1
     package.signed        |    1 |          0 |          1
     underwriting.decision |    1 |          0 |          1
     verification.result   |    3 |          0 |          3
    (4 rows)
    

verification.result and underwriting.decision arrive on the sample's DOMAIN
topics through @MaestroSignalListener, with a traceparent header present on
the record — and land with trace_context NULL. That is the defect.

document.uploaded and package.signed are NOT counter-evidence: they enter
through the REST API, not Kafka, so they have no inbound header to extract
and NULL is correct for them.
